course: dictionary
lesson: 5221
title: A jeep, and a brand name with a capital
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A jeep is a noun, a thing you can point to. It is a small, strong motor vehicle with four-wheel drive, so it can grip and move over rough ground where ordinary cars get stuck. «Films like to show a T. rex racing a jeep» tells you a jeep is built to go fast over bumpy land.
explain: Use the clues around the word. Racing suggests speed, and the jeep is racing a dinosaur, so it must be a vehicle. Test your meaning by putting it into the sentence: "a small, strong vehicle for rough ground" fits, so you are on the right track.
explain: Look at the letters. A small j means the vehicle in general. A capital J, as in Jeep, is a brand name, the name a company gives its own product, like the name of a shop or a sweet. Jeeps is the plural, so one jeep, two jeeps.
fact: A jeep is a small, strong motor vehicle with four-wheel drive, made for rough ground.
fact: The jeep was first made for the US Army in the Second World War.
fact: Jeep is also a brand name, so it is written with a capital J when it names the brand.
fact: A safari jeep is a jeep used on safari trips, where people travel to see wild animals.
fact: The plural of jeep is jeeps: one jeep, two jeeps.
exercise: «Films like to show a T. rex racing a jeep.» What is a jeep? | *A small, strong vehicle for rough ground | A type of dinosaur | A bird that can run very fast | A large boat for the sea
exercise: «The jeep bumped over the stones.» Find and copy the word that names a vehicle. | bumped | over | *jeep | stones
exercise: Which spelling shows the brand name Jeep? | *Jeep | a jeep | jeeps | jeep
test: What does four-wheel drive mean for a jeep? | The car has four doors and a big boot | Four drivers take turns at the wheel | Only two wheels turn, so the car is slow | *All four wheels can pull the car, so it can drive over rough ground
test: Which is the plural of jeep? | jeep's | jeepes | *jeeps | jeepies
exam: What is a safari jeep most likely used for? | *Driving across wild places to see animals | Printing books in a factory | Cooking dinner for the family | Sailing across the sea
